---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Why Rust — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="Rust for everything, or Rust for the engine and something else for the UI? The trade-offs I weighed and why I built LightPhotos entirely in Rust."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-06-28">June 28, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Why Rust</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          Rust for everything, or Rust for the engine and something else for
          the UI? The trade-offs I weighed and why I built LightPhotos
          entirely in Rust.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <h2>A language with a built-in reviewer</h2>
      <p>
        Rust is a fascinating language. It has the power of C++, and it comes
        with a strict code reviewer that stops at anything that looks off.
      </p>
      <p>
        That doesn't automatically make it the right choice for a photo editor.
        Before I wrote much code, I had a lot of questions.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>Is Rust a good choice for LightPhotos at all?</li>
        <li>
          Should I write only the most critical piece, the image engine, in
          Rust, and build the UI with a web frontend like React?
        </li>
        <li>Or should I build everything in Rust?</li>
        <li>Does Rust even have a decent UI library?</li>
        <li>
          Or should I build the UI in Swift so the app feels more like a Mac
          app?
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        There were two more things on my mind. Eventually I want a WASM
        version, so I can edit my photos the same way in a browser without
        sitting in front of my Mac. And what if I later want a Windows or Linux
        version?
      </p>

      <p>It came down to two shapes for the app.</p>

      <figure class="lp-arch">
        <div class="lp-arch-panel">
          <p class="lp-arch-label">Option 1 · All Rust</p>
          <div class="lp-arch-process">
            <span class="lp-arch-process-name">One Rust app</span>
            <div class="lp-arch-box">UI module</div>
            <div class="lp-arch-link">function calls</div>
            <div class="lp-arch-box lp-arch-box-core">Core engine</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-arch-panel">
          <p class="lp-arch-label">Option 2 · Split</p>
          <div class="lp-arch-process">
            <span class="lp-arch-process-name">Electron</span>
            <div class="lp-arch-box">React UI</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lp-arch-link lp-arch-link-cross">
            events &darr;&ensp;pixels &uarr;
          </div>
          <div class="lp-arch-process">
            <span class="lp-arch-process-name">Rust</span>
            <div class="lp-arch-box lp-arch-box-core">Core engine</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          With all Rust, the UI calls the engine directly. With the split, every
          slider drag and every preview crosses between two runtimes.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Everything in Rust</h2>
      <p>What I'd gain:</p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li><strong>One language</strong> for the whole app.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>No image data crossing runtimes.</strong> Pixels never have
          to be copied from one runtime to another.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No user events crossing runtimes.</strong> A slider drag
          goes straight to the code that handles it.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Faster performance.</strong></li>
        <li>
          <strong>Probably fewer dependencies and a smaller binary,</strong>
          and probably a simpler app to ship.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>What I'd lose:</p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Looks.</strong> Rust doesn't come with a UI toolkit of its
          own. The community libraries work, but the result doesn't look as
          slick as a well-styled web page or a native Mac app.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accessibility.</strong> Browsers give you good accessibility
          support for free. Rust UI libraries don't match it yet.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Rust for the engine, another language for the UI</h2>
      <p>What I'd gain:</p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>The best of each ecosystem,</strong> which should make the
          app faster to develop.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Full control over the look.</strong> With CSS I could tweak
          every detail of the app.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>What I'd lose:</p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Keeping two systems in sync.</strong> Every user action in
          the UI has to turn into work the engine performs, and the results
          have to come back.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>External dependencies.</strong> A web UI on the Mac means
          shipping an Electron app.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What about Swift?</h2>
      <p>
        A Swift UI would feel the most at home on a Mac. But it has the same
        split as the second option, with Swift talking to a Rust engine, and
        Swift doesn't come with me to the browser. A WASM version would need a
        second UI.
      </p>
      <p>
        Swift also ties the UI to Apple's platforms. A Windows or Linux version
        would need yet another UI. The Rust UI libraries and Electron both
        already run on all three desktop systems.
      </p>

      <h2>Side by side</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">All Rust</th>
              <th scope="col">Rust + React</th>
              <th scope="col">Rust + Swift</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Languages</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1</td>
              <td>2</td>
              <td>2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Engine and UI to keep in sync</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">No</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Extra runtime to ship</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">None</td>
              <td>Electron</td>
              <td>None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Look</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Plain</td>
              <td>Anything CSS can do</td>
              <td>Native Mac</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Accessibility</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Limited</td>
              <td>Browser-grade</td>
              <td>Native Mac</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Browser (WASM) version</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Same code</td>
              <td>Engine to WASM, UI as is</td>
              <td>Needs a second UI</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Windows and Linux</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Same code</td>
              <td>Same code</td>
              <td>Needs another UI</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Why I chose all Rust</h2>
      <p>
        Two things decided it. The first was the sync problem. Splitting the
        app into an engine and a UI sounds good on paper, but I kept wondering
        whether it would really work in practice.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        When the two sides disagree, the bug doesn't live in either one. It
        lives in the gap between them.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        I also do a lot of this work with coding agents. I wasn't confident an
        agent could debug a subtle mismatch between two systems written in
        different languages.
      </p>
      <p>
        And this isn't a typical web app with a backend server and a frontend
        UI. In a web app a little latency is expected. You click a button and
        wait a moment for the server to answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        In a photo editor, drag, zoom and pan are continuous. Every movement of
        a slider or the mouse has to reach the engine and come back as a new
        image right away, with no lag. Keeping two systems in sync at that
        speed is much harder than answering the occasional request.
      </p>
      <p>
        With one language and one runtime, there is no gap for that kind of bug
        to hide in, and nothing in the way of a real-time update.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second was performance. I want LightPhotos to be extremely fast and
        to use no more memory than it needs. With everything in Rust, there is
        no second runtime to load and no copies of the image passed between
        them.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I built everything in Rust. The UI may not be the slickest, but the
        whole app lives in one language, and that's a trade I'm happy with.
      </p>

      <h2>Leaving the door open</h2>
      <p>
        Even with everything in one language, I keep the UI logic and the core
        photo editing in separate modules. If I change my mind later and want a
        different UI, I can reuse most of the core as it is.
      </p>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Write the trade-offs down.</strong> Listing what each option
          gains and loses forced me to see what I didn't know. I dealt with
          those unknowns first, before they could surprise me later.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Trust the agents, but make it easy to step in.</strong>
          Agents do most of the work, but sometimes they get stuck playing
          whack-a-mole, fixing one symptom only for another to pop up. When
          that happens, I need a design simple enough that I can find the real
          problem myself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Think a few steps ahead.</strong> Swift would have been a
          fine choice for a Mac-only app. Thinking about the browser, Windows
          and Linux ruled it out.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep the logic separate and the code clean as I go.</strong>
          The core logic lives in its own module, apart from the UI. I also
          clean up sloppy, AI-generated code as it comes in, instead of
          letting it pile up.
        </li>
      </ul>

